<h2 id="finish-heading">Surface Finish Options</h2>
<h3>Mill Finish (2B)</h3>
<p>Mill finish is the default surface condition from the steel mill. It has a smooth, slightly reflective appearance. Mill finish is acceptable for most food conveyor applications where the frame does not directly contact the product. It costs the least because no additional finishing is required after fabrication. Most food-grade conveyors use mill-finish frames with sanitary welds and design features for cleanability.</p>
<h3>#4 Brushed Finish</h3>
<p>#4 finish is a directional brushed (satin) finish produced by polishing with progressively finer abrasives. It produces a uniform, matte appearance with visible grain lines. #4 finish is the most common specified finish for food and pharmaceutical equipment because it provides a smoother surface than mill finish (reducing bacterial attachment) while being less expensive than electropolish. Surface roughness: approximately 25-35 Ra (microinches).</p>
<h3>Electropolished Finish</h3>
<p>Electropolishing removes 0.0002"-0.001" of surface metal through an electrochemical process, dissolving peaks and smoothing micro-crevices. The result is an ultra-smooth, highly reflective surface with roughness below 15-20 Ra (microinches). Electropolished surfaces resist bacterial adhesion, clean faster, and require less aggressive cleaning chemicals. Electropolishing is specified in pharmaceutical cleanrooms, biotech manufacturing, and high-hygiene food applications (dairy, infant formula, ready-to-eat). The process adds significant cost to the conveyor frame.</p>
<h3>Bead-Blasted Finish</h3>
<p>Bead blasting creates a uniform matte texture by bombarding the surface with glass or ceramic beads. It produces a non-directional, low-reflectivity surface that hides minor scratches and handling marks. Bead-blasted surfaces are specified in pharmaceutical and medical device environments where glare from reflective surfaces is undesirable. Surface roughness is typically higher than #4 finish, so bead blasting is not preferred for ultra-sanitary applications.</p>
<h2 id="sanitary-heading">Sanitary Frame Design Principles</h2>
<p>A stainless steel frame alone does not make a sanitary conveyor. <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">Sanitary conveyor design</a> requires specific construction principles that eliminate bacterial harborage, facilitate cleaning, and prevent contamination:</p>
<p><strong>Continuous welds (no gaps):</strong> All frame joints must be continuously welded and ground smooth. Skip welds (intermittent welds) create crevices where bacteria, moisture, and product residue accumulate. Weld quality inspection (visual and, for critical applications, radiographic) verifies complete penetration and smooth surfaces.</p>
<p><strong>Sloped surfaces:</strong> All horizontal surfaces should slope at least 3-5 degrees to drain water and cleaning solutions. Standing water harbors bacteria. Flat ledges, brackets, and gussets that trap water must be eliminated or sloped. Frame cross-members use tubular profiles instead of angle iron to eliminate flat water-trapping surfaces.</p>
<p><strong>Open frame construction:</strong> Enclosed channels (C-channel, box tube) trap debris and are difficult to inspect and clean. Sanitary frames use open profiles that allow visual inspection and access for cleaning all surfaces. Stand-offs and spacers create gaps between components for water drainage and air drying.</p>
<p><strong>Sealed bearings and fasteners:</strong> Bearings use sealed housings that prevent grease migration into the product zone. Fasteners use smooth-head bolts instead of hex-head bolts (fewer crevices). All fasteners are stainless steel to prevent rust contamination. Captured (captive) fasteners prevent hardware from falling into the product stream.</p>
<p><strong>Tool-less belt access:</strong> Quick-release mechanisms allow operators to remove the belt for cleaning without tools. Hinged side frames swing open for full access to the belt underside, return run, and frame interior. Dorner, mk North America, and Nercon manufacture sanitary stainless conveyors with tool-less belt removal.</p>
<p><strong>Rounded corners and radii:</strong> Sharp internal corners trap debris and are impossible to clean effectively. Sanitary design specifies minimum internal radii (typically 1/4" or larger) at all frame corner transitions. External corners are rounded to prevent injury and product damage.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Stainless Steel Conveyor Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Food processing:</strong> Every conveyor in the product zone of a food processing facility should be stainless steel. Meat, poultry, dairy, bakery, produce, snack food, and prepared food lines all require stainless frames that withstand daily washdown. 304 stainless is standard for most food applications. 316 is required for seafood, brine, and acidic food environments.</p>
<p><strong>Beverage packaging:</strong> <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/beverage/">Tabletop chain conveyors</a> in beverage plants use stainless steel chains and frames to handle pasteurizer heat and daily washdown. Stainless steel resists the acidity of fruit juices, carbonated beverages, and dairy products. Wet sections (fill rooms, pasteurizer tunnels) require 316 stainless in high-chloride water areas.</p>
<p><strong>Pharmaceutical manufacturing:</strong> cGMP (current Good Manufacturing Practice) facilities require stainless steel conveyors in all production, packaging, and warehousing areas. Electropolished 316L stainless is standard in cleanroom and aseptic processing areas. <a href="/conveyor-types/vacuum-conveyors/">Vacuum conveyors</a> and <a href="/conveyor-types/pneumatic-conveyors/">pneumatic systems</a> use stainless steel tubing and receivers for powder transfer between process steps.</p>
<p><strong>Chemical processing:</strong> Conveyors handling chemical products, intermediates, and raw materials in chemical plants use 316 or higher-grade stainless to resist chemical attack. Alloy selection depends on specific chemicals, concentrations, and temperatures. Chemical compatibility charts from steel suppliers (Outokumpu, Sandvik) guide alloy selection.</p>
<p><strong>Outdoor and washdown environments:</strong> Any conveyor in a wet or outdoor environment benefits from stainless steel construction. Car washes, outdoor bottling lines, water treatment plants, and dock areas that see rain and humidity corrode carbon steel rapidly. Stainless steel eliminates the ongoing repainting and rust maintenance that carbon steel requires in these environments.</p>
<h2 id="cost-heading">Cost Considerations</h2>
<p>Stainless steel conveyors cost 40-80% more than equivalent carbon steel conveyors. The premium varies by alloy grade, amount of stainless used, and surface finish requirements. However, the total cost of ownership often favors stainless in corrosive environments:</p>
<p><strong>Material cost:</strong> 304 stainless steel costs roughly 2-3x carbon steel per pound. 316 adds another 20-30% over 304. These are material costs. The fabricated conveyor price increase is smaller because labor, motors, belts, and controls cost the same regardless of frame material.</p>
<p><strong>Elimination of painting:</strong> Carbon steel conveyors require primer and topcoat. In corrosive environments, paint systems fail, requiring touch-up and repainting every 1-3 years. Stainless steel never needs painting, eliminating ongoing coating maintenance.</p>
<p><strong>Longer service life:</strong> In wet environments, carbon steel conveyors corrode from within, weakening the frame structure. A painted carbon steel conveyor in a washdown food plant may last 5-8 years before structural corrosion requires replacement. A stainless steel conveyor in the same environment lasts 15-25+ years. The lifecycle cost advantage of stainless is substantial in corrosive environments.</p>
<p><strong>Hybrid construction:</strong> To reduce cost, some manufacturers offer hybrid conveyors with stainless steel product-contact surfaces and carbon steel structural members. The product zone uses stainless, while non-contact areas (legs, drive housing, support gussets) use painted carbon steel. This reduces material cost by 30-50% compared to all-stainless construction while maintaining sanitary compliance in the product zone.</p>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a stainless steel conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A conveyor using stainless steel alloys for its frame, rails, legs, and hardware. Resists corrosion from washdown, chemicals, and acids. Meets FDA, USDA, and 3-A sanitary standards. Available in 304 (general food/pharma) and 316 (chloride/acid-resistant) grades.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">304 vs 316 stainless steel?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>304: standard for most food/pharma. 18% chromium, 8% nickel. Handles water, mild chemicals, standard CIP. 316: adds 2-3% molybdenum for chloride and acid resistance. Required for seafood, brine, coastal, and chemical environments. 316 costs 20-30% more than 304.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What is electropolished stainless steel?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>An electrochemical process that removes a thin surface layer, creating an ultra-smooth, mirror-like finish. Fewer micro-crevices for bacteria. Easier and faster to clean. Specified for pharmaceutical cleanrooms, biotech, and high-hygiene food (dairy, infant formula). Adds significant cost over #4 brushed finish.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">Stainless vs. carbon steel conveyor cost?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Stainless costs 40-80% more upfront. However, stainless eliminates repainting, resists corrosion-related failures, and lasts 15-25+ years in washdown environments vs. 5-8 years for painted carbon steel. Lifecycle cost often favors stainless in wet or corrosive environments. Hybrid construction (stainless contact surfaces, carbon steel structure) saves 30-50%.</p>

</div>
